Z||000000||2M4 Dale||04-29-2000||12:58 AM||||I just put a set in my 87 2M4. We'll see how they perform tommorow. I put a set of the Fiero Store Plugwires on Wednesday (Red Ones...WOW) and they seemed to help the noise in my sterio and car ran a little smoother too !||||reg||6
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-29-2000||01:38 AM||||Yup.... I recently changed plugs in my V6 with rapidfires.  It didn't really make any more of a difference than putting regular plugs normally does when replacing an old set.  It's piece of mind to know that you've got RAPIDFIRES though.  It kinda has a nice ring to it.... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||04-29-2000||01:38 AM||||You will here alot of opinions on this subject. some good ..some bad. I can tell you that I placed rapid fires in ...||||reg||1
Z||000003||RobertVol||04-29-2000||01:45 AM||||You will here alot of opinions on this subject. some good ..some bad. I can tell you that I placed rapid fires in ...<P>my 88GT with low miles after I purchased ....the car I purchased Had been in storage for years so naturally I changed the plugs even though low miles ( 28k). The change made a noticable difference. Dont know if it was because they needed to be changed anyway or because they were a good replacement product to use. But the car ran good before replacing but ran even better after. The plugs I replaced were not that bad even though being in storage for so long. The difference to me was in steady acceleration. It was alot smoother. I didnt get that little hick up fieros sometimes get in the v-6's. but in a four ? hmmm guess you will have to wait 'n see.   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||04-29-2000||11:52 AM||||I have had rapid fires in my v6 fiero for about 12,000 miles and all is well. I replaced some autolites (junk) that were brand new and misfiring.||||reg||14
Z||000005||wkayl||04-29-2000||10:03 PM||||I put in Bosh Platnum 4s.  I like them so far (about 10,000 miles in the last 11 months).||||reg||1
Z||000006||2M4 Dale||04-29-2000||11:14 PM||||Well my iron duck runs great with the rapid fires. The little miss I had is gone. It even seems to pull better at a lower RPM. BUT it seems as though the car runs a little rougher when cold. Might be my imagination though.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Gokart||04-30-2000||05:53 AM||||I would've tried those if it wasn't for the Bosch platinum+4's. I got about 10,000 on my too and she runs like the day I put'em in <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||2
Z||000008||Master_Sushi||04-30-2000||12:38 PM||||this might be a little O-T,but I though it was that best thread to put it in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.Is it true that the engine cradle has to be droped in the V6 to change sparkplugs? <P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000009||TheFiero64||04-30-2000||02:30 PM||||No the cradle doesn't have to be dropped. There is enough room to get the wrench and your hand in there. It is a lot easier than a lot of late model cars w/front wheel drive I've tried. Who told you that? They pulled your chain <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000010||Phaeton||04-30-2000||02:44 PM||||I have had several folks tell me the plugs can be changed with the engine in the Fiero.<BR>But I have not seen this happen and cannot get details of how or even what kind of wrench.<BR>My plugs have been changed twice since owning the Fiero, both times when the engine was out for a new clutch (plugs last longer than clutches with me).<BR>I do not consider myself inept (others may) but I cannot even touch two of the front plugs, much less get a wrench to them.<BR>Oh well, needs a clutch again so new plugs are in order, going to use rapidfire this time because they have the cool name and don't sound like they will hurt performance.||||reg||12
Z||000011||Standard||05-01-2000||01:07 AM||||Take the decklid off.  Real easy (4 bolts, rfi sheild clip, and trunk release plug if you have it) and gives you lots more clearance.  A flexible extension help alot, just make sure you torque the plug straight.<P>It took me about a half-hour to 45 min's to get the front bank done my first time, not including time to get the decklid off.  About 10 min for the front bank.  It's not too hard to align the decklid, either.  Helps if you have a, well, helper, too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||wkayl||05-01-2000||11:08 AM||||There are 3 steps in changing plugs on V6 Fiero.<P>Step 1.  empty trunk<BR>Step 2.  stand in trunk<BR>Step 3.  reach in from left side of engine near fire wall.<P>Doing this you can reach all the way to the right side of the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000013||TheFiero64||05-01-2000||11:43 AM||||If you use wkayl's method you should not have any problem getting the plugs out. Even if you do have AC like my GT has. ||||reg||10
Z||000014||Standard||05-01-2000||02:33 PM||||Forgot about that part... I always stand in the trunk 'cause it's a lot more comfy than trying to reach over the fastback panels.. ||||reg||1
